<BaseLayout title="Fishponds, a Crater and the Milky Way — Huanyu Chen"
  description="A long weekend on Hawaiʻi Island: Kona and Hilo, ancient fishponds on the Kohala Coast, the floor of Kīlauea Iki, and a sunset above the clouds on Mauna Kea followed by the Milky Way.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · September 2025</p>
  <h1>Fishponds, a Crater and the Milky Way</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A long weekend on Hawaiʻi Island, from the Kona coast to 9,200 feet on Mauna Kea.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>4 days · September 11 – 14, 2025</strong><br />
    Kona → Waikoloa → across the Saddle to Hilo → the Kohala Coast → a sunset boat off Keauhou →
    the Kalahuipuaʻa fishponds → Kīlauea Iki → Mauna Kea → Kona<br />
    One rental car · both sides of the island in a day, twice · sea level to 9,200 feet in an afternoon ·
    one active volcano, one sacred mountain · about thirty photographs of the Milky Way
  </div>

  <p>
    This was a short one: four days on Hawaiʻi Island, the one everybody calls the Big Island, in the middle of
    September. We flew into Kona on a Thursday morning and flew out again on Sunday afternoon,
    with a rental car in between and an island bigger than all the other Hawaiian islands put together.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, everything below is rebuilt from the camera roll, which remembers where and
    when every photo was taken. It turns out to be a good diary. It is honest about how much of the weekend was spent
    eating, and it records the one day, Saturday, when the island showed us almost everything it has: ancient
    fishponds in the morning, a volcanic crater in the afternoon, a sunset above the clouds, and then the Milky Way.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Kona and the lava coast</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0911.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0911.webp'} alt="Map of Day 1: from the Kona airport south to Kailua-Kona for lunch and the KONA sign, then north along the coast to Waikoloa and dinner at ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: the Kona airport, lunch in Kailua-Kona, then up the coast to Waikoloa.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Arriving in Kona</h3>
  <p>
    We landed just before noon. The Kona airport is one of the nicest places in the country to arrive, because it
    barely has a building. The terminals are a row of low open-air pavilions under steep, peaked roofs, the kind of
    architecture that looks borrowed from a traditional hale, with big monkeypod trees spreading over the curb. You walk
    off the plane and straight into the warm air. There's no jet bridge and no long corridor, just sun, a breeze and the
    smell of hot asphalt and flowers. Within twenty minutes we had a rental car and were heading south into town.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-airport.webp'} alt="The Kona airport curb: a wide road, parked cars, low pavilion-style terminal roofs and huge monkeypod trees under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kona airport, 11:52 am. The terminal is mostly roof.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The land around the airport is a surprise if you expect Hawaiʻi to be green. This side of the island sits in the
    rain shadow of its big volcanoes, and most of what you see from the road is lava: rough black fields of it, some
    old enough to have grown a fuzz of golden grass, some still bare. The airport itself is built on a lava flow from
    Hualālai, the volcano that rises behind Kailua-Kona. The green arrives with the towns, the gardens and the trees
    people have planted.
  </p>

  <h3>Lunch in Kailua-Kona</h3>
  <p>
    First stop: lunch, in a small shopping center in <strong>Kailua-Kona</strong>. The table filled up quickly. There
    was a tall ube drink topped with a dome of purple ube ice cream, a bowl of grilled garlic shrimp on rice and salad,
    a whole fried fish scored into crispy slices and set on a nest of fried noodles, and a bowl of pho with an
    absolutely enormous beef rib standing up out of the broth like a mast. Ube, the purple yam, is everywhere in
    Hawaiʻi, a legacy of the islands' large Filipino community, and it turns everything it touches a slightly unreal
    shade of lavender.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ube-drink.webp'} alt="A tall milky drink with a scoop of purple ube ice cream under a clear dome lid, on a wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An ube drink, the first purple thing of the trip.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pho-rib.webp'} alt="A bowl of pho with herbs, lime and a giant beef rib bone standing up out of the broth." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pho, with a rib the size of a forearm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The garlic shrimp deserves a word. Garlic shrimp is the island cousin of the famous shrimp trucks of Oʻahu's North
    Shore: shell-on shrimp cooked hard in butter and a frankly irresponsible amount of garlic, served with a scoop of
    rice. Here it came grilled, with char marks, a creamy dressing and a salad underneath. The fried fish was the
    showpiece, though, cut so that every slice crisped up separately and the whole thing looked like a golden
    accordion.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'garlic-shrimp.webp'} alt="A bowl of grilled garlic shrimp on salad greens with a scoop of white rice." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Garlic shrimp.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fried-fish.webp'} alt="A whole fried fish scored into crispy slices, topped with green onion, on a bed of fried noodles." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The fried fish, scored like an accordion.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    After lunch we walked down to the waterfront on <strong>Aliʻi Drive</strong>, the old seafront road of
    Kailua-Kona. A century and a half ago this was a royal retreat: Hawaiian aliʻi kept homes along this shore, and
    Huliheʻe Palace, a summer palace of the Hawaiian royal family, still stands on the bay. Today the road is lined with
    shops, restaurants and the seawall, and at one end someone has planted the town's name in giant green topiary
    letters, decorated with oversized plumeria flowers. Behind them is the blue of Kailua Bay. It is the most
    photographed hedge on the island, and we did our part.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-sign.webp'} alt="Giant green topiary letters spelling KONA with large white-and-yellow plumeria flowers, the ocean behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The KONA sign on Aliʻi Drive, 2:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Up the coast to Waikoloa</h3>
  <p>
    From Kona the highway runs north for half an hour through nothing but lava. It is one of the strangest drives in
    the United States. The road is a thin gray line through black and brown flows that poured down from Hualālai and
    Mauna Loa, and people have spelled out messages along the shoulders in white coral stones on the dark rock. Then,
    suddenly, there is a turnoff, a ribbon of irrigated green, and you are in <strong>Waikoloa</strong>, where a resort
    community has been built into the lava, all lawns, palms and golf courses, with the black rock showing at every
    edge.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-roundabout.webp'} alt="A shaggy tree with a mass of drooping fronds standing alone on a grassy roundabout, with palms behind under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A roundabout in Waikoloa, with a tree that looks like it needs a haircut.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fresh-juice.webp'} alt="Two glasses of fresh juice, one orange and one pink, on a polished wooden table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fresh juice, 3:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first afternoon was slow. There was a stop for fresh juice, one orange and one pink, at a table made from a
    single slab of polished wood, while the sky over the mountains filled with cloud. Most of the island's weather happens in the afternoon, when the sea breeze pushes moist air
    up the slopes and it condenses into a line of cloud halfway up the volcanoes. From the coast you can watch it build
    hour by hour, while the beach stays in sun.
  </p>

  <h3>Dinner at ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay</h3>
  <p>
    Dinner was at <strong>Lava Lava Beach Club</strong>, right on the sand at ʻAnaehoʻomalu Bay. The bay is a long
    curve of salt-and-pepper sand, with old Hawaiian fishponds just behind the beach, and the restaurant is an
    open-air room with string lights, palm trunks and a view of the water going dark. We got there just after seven,
    as the last light faded behind the palms. By the time the food came, the room was lit only by the string lights and
    candles on the tables.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lava-lava-dinner.webp'} alt="Dinner on a ledge in an open-air restaurant: fried calamari, coconut shrimp with rice and an orchid, and a dish served in a hollowed half-pineapple." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Calamari, coconut shrimp, and dinner in a pineapple.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The main event came in a hollowed-out half pineapple, with grilled meat, mango and sauce piled into the shell. Next
    to it were coconut shrimp with rice and a purple orchid, and a basket of fried calamari. Then came dessert: a slice
    of pie with chocolate drizzle and another orchid, and a dome covered in toasted coconut flakes. On the islands,
    orchids are the garnish of choice. By the end of the weekend we had collected quite a few.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'dessert.webp'} alt="Two desserts seen from above: a slice of pie with chocolate drizzle and a purple orchid, and a round dessert covered in toasted coconut." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dessert, 8 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: Across the island and back</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0912.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0912.webp'} alt="Map of Day 2: from Waikoloa east across the island to Hilo for the farmers market and lunch, back to the Kohala Coast, then south to a sunset boat off Keauhou and dinner on Aliʻi Drive." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: across the Saddle to Hilo, back to the Kohala Coast, then south for the sunset.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Over the Saddle</h3>
  <p>
    Friday was a crossing. The Big Island has two very different sides: Kona in the west, dry, sunny and built on lava,
    and Hilo in the east, one of the rainiest cities in the United States. Between them stand two huge volcanoes, Mauna
    Kea to the north and Mauna Loa to the south, and the road between them crosses the high plateau known as the
    Saddle. We left Waikoloa late in the morning and climbed Waikoloa Road under a sky full of enormous cumulus clouds.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-road.webp'} alt="A two-lane road climbing through dry scrub under dramatic white cumulus clouds and wispy high cloud, with mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikoloa Road, 11:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'saddle-lava.webp'} alt="A rough black lava outcrop beside the road, with clouds piled over a distant mountain flank." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lava beside the road, and clouds over the mountain.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The landscape up there feels nothing like the postcards. It is open, high and empty, all dry grass, scrub and lava
    under a very big sky, more like the American West than the tropics. The clouds were the show: towers of white
    cumulus over the slopes, and above them high, feathery cirrus pulled into streaks by the wind. In about an hour the
    road crests the Saddle and drops down the windward side, the air turns damp, and the trees close in, green and
    dripping. By half past twelve we were in Hilo.
  </p>

  <h3>Hilo</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Hilo</strong> is the island's old town and its county seat, and it feels completely different from Kona.
    It is low, wooden and a little weathered, with a waterfront that was rebuilt as parks after tsunamis in 1946 and
    1960 destroyed the old bayfront. The downtown has kept its early-twentieth-century storefronts, and a lot of
    murals. The first photo in Hilo is of a white concrete building with a familiar pineapple house
    painted on its wall, complete with porthole windows and a little blue door. If you know, you know.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pineapple-mural.webp'} alt="A white concrete building with a mural of a cartoon pineapple house with a blue door and round windows." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Somebody lives in a pineapple in Hilo.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came the <strong>Hilo Farmers Market</strong>, a long, covered market hall on the corner of downtown, with
    murals of giant sliced fruit painted along the back wall and stalls piled high underneath. There were mountains of
    papaya, bananas in every size, pineapples, avocados, dragon fruit, rambutan, sweet potatoes and plenty of things we
    couldn't name. Hilo's rain is good for something: the wet windward side is where much of the island's fruit
    grows.
  </p>
  <p>
    We did not leave empty-handed. We bought far more fruit than two days could reasonably use, and all of it was
    really good: rambutan, the hairy red cousin of the lychee, sweet and translucent inside its spiky shell, and
    papaya so cheap it felt like a mistake.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-market.webp'} alt="The covered Hilo Farmers Market: rows of fruit and vegetable stalls under blue umbrellas, with a colorful mural of sliced fruit along the back wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hilo Farmers Market, 12:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Lunch was a few blocks away, downtown on Keawe Street, and it was the most tropical meal of the trip. The drinks
    came in a hollowed-out pineapple, topped with whipped cream, cocktail umbrellas and a straw, next to a tall glass
    of something orange and fruity with its own umbrella. A single rose stood in a vase on the table. For food there
    was a creamy pasta and a loco moco: rice topped with a hamburger patty, brown gravy and a fried
    egg. It was in the right place. The loco moco is generally credited to Hilo, where it was invented in the
    late 1940s as a cheap, filling plate for hungry teenagers.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pineapple-drink.webp'} alt="A drink served in a hollowed pineapple with whipped cream and straws, beside a rose in a small vase and an orange drink with a pink paper umbrella." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch drinks, Hilo style.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hilo-lunch.webp'} alt="Plates on a granite table seen from above: a creamy pasta, a rice plate with gravy and egg, and the pineapple drink." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pasta, and a loco moco.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Back to the Kohala Coast</h3>
  <p>
    Then we drove straight back across the island. By four we were on the <strong>Kohala Coast</strong>, north of
    Waikoloa, where we spent the second night at the <strong>Mauna Lani</strong> resort. The afternoon snack was a whole fresh durian, cracked open on an outdoor
    table. Durian is famous for its smell, which some people describe as heaven and others as a gas leak, and
    infamous enough that many hotels and public transit systems in Southeast Asia ban it outright. It does grow in
    Hawaiʻi, in small numbers, mostly on the wet side of this island. This one was eaten outside, which seemed only
    fair to everyone else.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'durian.webp'} alt="A spiky durian, cracked open to show a pale yellow segment of flesh, on a gray outdoor table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A fresh durian, 4 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kohala-lagoon.webp'} alt="A calm, shallow lagoon with a low lava reef, palms on the far shore and heavy gray clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A lagoon on the Kohala Coast under a gray sky.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The weather had turned. At a quarter to five the sky over the coast was a heavy gray ceiling, and the shallow
    lagoon by the beach was calm and the color of pewter, with a low reef of black lava sitting in it like a sleeping
    animal. It is a good reminder that even the dry side of the island has its moods. The clouds that build over the
    mountains every afternoon sometimes spill down to the coast, and for an hour or so everything goes soft and quiet.
  </p>

  <h3>A sunset boat off Keauhou</h3>
  <p>
    Then we drove south again, past Kona, for the evening's plan: a boat out of <strong>Keauhou</strong>, south of
    Kailua-Kona. It was almost certainly the manta-ray night snorkel. By 6:25 we were in wetsuits on the deck of a boat heading out from the coast, and the sky was doing
    something remarkable. The gray ceiling hadn't gone anywhere, but it ended a little above the horizon, and the sun
    slipped into the gap between cloud and sea. The result was a single blazing band of orange, with a sailboat
    silhouetted against it and the rest of the sky still gray.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boat-sunset-1.webp'} alt="The rail and deck of a boat at sea, with a band of gold sunset light under gray clouds and a small sailboat on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Heading out, 6:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'boat-sunset-2.webp'} alt="A brilliant orange stripe of sunset between dark clouds and a dark sea, seen over a boat railing and coiled ropes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sky at 6:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    For a few minutes the orange stripe grew brighter and redder. The photos stop at 6:44, which on a boat, at dusk, in a wetsuit, usually means the phone has gone somewhere
    dry. What happened next is not in the camera roll.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is easy enough to guess, though. The Kona coast is one of the most reliable places in the world to see reef
    manta rays at night, and the boats out of Keauhou head for a spot just off the coast where they gather after
    dark. The trick is light: the boats shine lights into the water, the lights draw plankton, and the plankton draws
    the mantas, which glide up out of the dark with their mouths wide open and loop and somersault through the beam,
    sometimes just below the snorkelers floating at the surface.
  </p>

  <h3>Thai food and a glowing sign</h3>
  <p>
    Dinner was late, on Aliʻi Drive in Kailua-Kona, at a Thai restaurant with metal tables: a sampler plate of curry,
    fried chicken wings and a beef salad with cucumbers, then a plate of drunken noodles with beef and green beans, and
    finally a warm bowl of taro and coconut tapioca, purple swirled through white. After a boat at sunset, there is
    nothing better than hot food. Walking back to the car, we passed the KONA topiary again. At night it is wrapped in
    thousands of tiny white lights, glowing against the dark parking lot like a giant hedge-shaped lantern.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'thai-plate.webp'} alt="A divided plate with a bowl of curry, fried chicken wings, and a beef salad with cucumbers, on a metal table." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Thai on Aliʻi Drive, 8:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'taro-dessert.webp'} alt="A small bowl of white coconut tapioca swirled with purple taro, with a spoon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Taro and coconut tapioca.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    It is a cheerful sight: a small town's name, spelled out in lights, at nine at night, with the sea invisible
    behind it but very much audible. The sign had changed completely in seven hours, from green and plumeria in the
    afternoon sun to a glittering outline in the dark. Then it was another drive north, back up the lava coast to the
    Kohala Coast.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-sign-night.webp'} alt="The KONA topiary letters covered in glowing white lights at night, in an empty parking lot." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same sign, 9:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few bags of fruit and snacks from town came back with us for later. Saturday would need an early start.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 3: Fishponds, a crater and the Milky Way</h2>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0913.webp'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0913.webp'} alt="Map of Days 3 and 4: the Kalahuipuaʻa fishponds on the Kohala Coast, then across the island to Kīlauea Iki and the Devastation Trail, north to Mauna Kea for sunset and the Milky Way, a night in Waikoloa, and Kailua-Kona and the airport on Sunday." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Days 3–4: fishponds, Kīlauea Iki, Mauna Kea, and back to Kona for the flight home.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Saturday was the day this trip is really about. Looking at the map afterwards, it is a slightly absurd loop: a
    morning walk at sea level on the northwest coast, a drive all the way across the island to the volcano in the
    southeast, then up to 9,200 feet on Mauna Kea for sunset and the stars, and back down to the coast at nine at night.
    Four very different landscapes, one long day.
  </p>

  <h3>Mauna Lani</h3>
  <p>
    Before the shore, the hotel itself, because it was the most beautiful building of the trip. The
    <strong>Mauna Lani</strong> is built around a huge open-air atrium. The guest floors step back on both sides
    like terraces, and the space between them, open to the sky, is filled with a garden: tall palms, ferns and
    a wooden walkway running down the middle toward the lobby. In the morning, looking down from one of the upper
    walkways, it felt less like a hotel corridor than like standing on the rim of a small green canyon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-atrium.webp'} alt="Looking down into a tall open-air hotel atrium: terraced balconies on both sides, palms and ferns below, and a wooden walkway leading to a lattice pavilion." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The atrium from an upper walkway, 8:28 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Down at the bottom, the garden is thick enough to get lost in for a minute, with stepping-stone paths between the
    palm trunks. Everything else is wood: slatted screens, lattice pavilions and dark plank floors, so the whole
    building feels shaded and open to the breeze.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-palms.webp'} alt="A dense garden of palms and ferns inside the atrium, with stepping-stone paths and a wooden deck." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The garden inside the atrium.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-bridge.webp'} alt="A wooden staircase with dark railings leading down into an open lobby, with a long canoe and a lattice pavilion beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Stairs down to the lobby, 8:43 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the middle of the lobby sits an old outrigger canoe. Its plaque says it was carved from koa with traditional
    tools more than a hundred years ago, in the fishing village of Miloliʻi: an <em>ʻōpelu</em> canoe for four
    paddlers, 28 feet long, able to carry up to 2,000 pounds of fish. Behind it, under hanging woven lamps, are
    low sofas facing the garden. I liked the design very much, and I would happily recommend the place to anyone
    visiting this coast.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-canoe.webp'} alt="A long dark koa outrigger canoe with its curved wooden outrigger, on display on a plank floor in an open lobby with lattice screens." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Kalāhuipuaʻa canoe in the lobby.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-lani-lobby.webp'} alt="Low white sofas with gray and yellow cushions under black woven pendant lamps, looking out over the lobby and the atrium." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lobby lounge, 8:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Morning on the shore</h3>
  <p>
    The morning was the clearest of the trip. The gray of the day before had gone, and at a quarter to nine the sky was
    a deep, saturated blue with a few puffy clouds, and the light was strong enough to make the palms throw sharp
    shadows across the paths. We walked the shoreline path along the beach, where lawns run down to a strip of
    sand and then black lava rock and the sea. Under Hawaiian law the shoreline is public, and paths like this one
    let anybody walk along it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shoreline-path.webp'} alt="A paved path curving along a sandy shoreline with tall palms, bright lawns and the blue sea under a clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The shoreline path, 8:49 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'morning-lawn.webp'} alt="A lawn in deep shade under a large tree, opening onto a pond, trees and palms in bright sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The edge of the fishponds, seen from under a tree.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The fishponds of Kalahuipuaʻa</h3>
  <p>
    Just behind the shore is the reason to come to this stretch of coast: the <strong>Kalahuipuaʻa fishponds</strong>,
    a cluster of ancient Hawaiian fishponds now protected as a historic park with a walking trail. A bronze plaque at
    the edge of the largest pond, <em>Na Loko Iʻa O Kalahuipuaʻa</em>, shows the shape of the system: several ponds,
    some open and some enclosed, threaded through the lava a few steps from the ocean. They are still fed the way they
    always were, by fresh water seeping up through the porous lava from the mountains and mixing with seawater.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-plaque.webp'} alt="A bronze plaque titled Na Loko Iʻa O Kalahuipuaʻa with a map of the fishponds, in front of a wide pond ringed by palms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>Na Loko Iʻa O Kalahuipuaʻa</em>, the fishponds of Kalahuipuaʻa.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-boardwalk.webp'} alt="A white wooden boardwalk running along a stone wall between a pond and the ocean, with green shrubs in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The boardwalk between pond and sea.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Hawaiian fishponds, <em>loko iʻa</em>, were one of the great engineering achievements of old Hawaiʻi: aquaculture
    practiced at scale centuries before Europeans arrived. The ponds were connected to the sea through
    <em>mākāhā</em>, sluice gates made of wooden slats. Young fish could swim in through the gaps, feed and grow in the
    rich brackish water, and then find themselves too big to swim back out. The ponds raised mullet and milkfish, and
    in Hawaiian tradition the best of the harvest went to the aliʻi, the chiefs. A boardwalk crosses the wall between
    one pond and the ocean, so you stand with still water on one side and surf on the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    Further along, the trail passes a small A-frame hale with a thatched roof, set in a field of tall grass beside a
    shallow pond. Kalahuipuaʻa was once a fishing village, and the people who tended the ponds lived beside them in
    houses much like it. Around it, the grass grows straight out of
    the lava, and the shallow ponds hold perfect reflections of the palms and clouds.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-hale.webp'} alt="A small thatched A-frame hale at the edge of a grassy field with a shallow pond and tall palms under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A thatched hale beside the ponds.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-marsh.webp'} alt="A shallow marshy pond reflecting palms and clouds, with green grass and a palm trunk in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Reflections, 9:30 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The ponds are ringed by black lava, and the contrast is the thing you remember. The water is calm and dark blue,
    the rock around it is rough and black, and the grass and palms are an almost fluorescent green. In one pond the
    lava edge breaks up into little bays and points, and the reflections of the white clouds sit in the water like
    floating islands. Some of the smaller ponds are so still they look like holes cut into the sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-lava.webp'} alt="A calm pond reflecting white clouds, edged in the foreground by rough black lava rock, with palms and buildings on the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A pond framed by lava.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The path loops between the ponds under coconut palms, past the biggest pond, where a single palm has grown out
    sideways from the bank and lies almost flat over the water, its crown reaching up again at the end. A few
    white water birds stood in the shallows that morning. It is a strange and wonderful thing to find a place like this a few minutes' walk from the resort lawns: a working landscape from
    centuries ago, still holding water, still holding fish.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-palms.webp'} alt="A paved path curving beside a large blue pond, with tall coconut palm trunks leaning over the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The trail around the largest pond.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-leaning-palm.webp'} alt="A palm tree growing out almost horizontally over a calm pond, its crown curving up at the end." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A palm that decided to grow sideways.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By a quarter to ten we were at the far end of the trail, where a grove of palms stands right at the water's edge
    on a bank of black rock. The trunks lean in every direction, and the pond behind them is so still that the grove
    appears twice. Then we walked back, had a picnic lunch of rotisserie chicken from a grocery store, and got ready for the long drive.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'fishpond-grove.webp'} alt="A grove of coconut palms with curved trunks standing on black lava at the edge of a calm, reflective pond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The palm grove at the end of the trail, 9:37 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>To the volcano</h3>
  <p>
    At two we were back on Waikoloa Road, climbing inland. Mauna Kea was ahead of us, its upper slopes hidden by a
    long, flat band of cloud, the usual afternoon cloud cap, like a scarf around its neck. The route to the volcano
    runs across the Saddle again and then down the windward side and south, through Hilo and up into the rainforest
    around Kīlauea. It took us about two hours, from sun and lava to mist and ferns.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'inland-drive.webp'} alt="Looking inland from Waikoloa Road: dry hills and scattered houses under a long band of white cloud lying across the mountain slopes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Heading inland under the cloud cap, 1:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We reached <strong>Hawaiʻi Volcanoes National Park</strong> at a quarter to four, at the trailhead for the
    <strong>Devastation Trail</strong>. The sign at the start tells the story in its title: <em>Keoneʻāhelelei</em>,
    "a scorching rain of cinder". In 1959, the crater next door, Kīlauea Iki, erupted, and its lava fountains shot up
    to about 1,900 feet, the highest ever measured in Hawaiʻi. The wind carried the falling cinder and pumice
    southwest, and it buried the forest here under a blanket of black, killing the trees and leaving a field of bare
    trunks sticking out of the cinders.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'devastation-sign.webp'} alt="A national park trail sign titled Keoneʻāhelelei, A Scorching Rain of Cinder, with a photo of a lava fountain and a map of the Devastation Area." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The trailhead sign: <em>Keoneʻāhelelei</em>.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'devastation-trail.webp'} alt="A paved path across a field of dark cinder, with ʻōhiʻa trees and shrubs regrowing at its edge under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Devastation Trail, sixty-six years on.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Sixty-six years later, the forest is coming back. The trail is a paved path across a field of dark cinder, and
    the edges are already green: ʻōhiʻa trees, the hardy native tree that is usually the first to colonize new lava,
    shrubs and ferns, with the bleached trunks of the old dead forest still standing among them. Red-tipped seedlings
    poke up through the cinder. It is the most literal walk through ecological succession you will ever take, with a
    clear before and after.
  </p>

  <h3>Kīlauea Iki</h3>
  <p>
    Then the trees opened, and there was <strong>Kīlauea Iki</strong>, "little Kīlauea", the pit crater just east of
    the main caldera. It is about a mile long and four hundred feet deep, with steep walls covered in rainforest, and
    a flat gray floor. That floor is the top of the lava lake that filled the crater in 1959. Lava from the fountain
    poured into the crater and pooled, more than four hundred feet deep, and it took decades for the lake to cool and
    solidify all the way through.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-iki-ohia.webp'} alt="The gray floor of Kīlauea Iki crater seen through the branches of an ʻōhiʻa tree, with forested crater walls behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Kīlauea Iki through the ʻōhiʻa, 3:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-iki-floor.jpg'} alt="Close view of the cracked, ridged gray lava floor of Kīlauea Iki crater, with the forested crater wall behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The crater floor: a frozen lava lake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From the rim you can see how it set. The surface is cracked into plates and ridged where the crust buckled as it
    cooled and the lake beneath it drained and shifted. A long, faint line across it marks the trail that crosses the
    floor. The walls around it are completely green, ʻōhiʻa and tree ferns all the way down. The contrast,
    living forest around a gray floor of rock that was liquid within living memory, is like nothing else.
  </p>
  <p>
    The clouds were moving fast over the crater that afternoon, and their shadows slid across the floor, so the gray
    lava kept changing from silver to charcoal. From the rim it looked enormous and absolutely silent. We walked on
    along the <strong>Crater Rim Trail</strong> through the forest to the Steam Vents, where groundwater seeping down
    onto hot rock rises again as steam from cracks in the ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    Of the volcano's famous glow there was little to none that day, just smoke and vog drifting over the caldera.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kilauea-iki-wide.webp'} alt="A wide view of Kīlauea Iki crater under bright, moving clouds, with forest in the foreground and around the crater walls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole crater, under moving clouds.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'crater-rim-forest.webp'} alt="Dark rainforest trees on the Crater Rim Trail framing a glimpse of the gray crater beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Crater Rim Trail, 4:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By half past four we were back at the car, because we had another appointment. The sun sets at around half past
    six in mid-September, and Mauna Kea was the better part of two hours away.
  </p>

  <h3>Sunset above the clouds</h3>
  <p>
    We made it with almost no time to spare. At 6:16 we were at the <strong>Mauna Kea Visitor Information
    Station</strong>, the Onizuka Center, at about 9,200 feet on the mountain's south slope. This was as high as we
    went: the summit was closed that day, and our 2WD rental car wasn't allowed above the Visitor Station anyway, since
    the summit road requires four-wheel drive. Below us, filling the whole western horizon,
    was the cloud layer, the same clouds that had capped the mountain in the afternoon, now seen from above, flat and
    white like a frozen sea. The sky above was clear. The sun was going down behind it in a band of gold.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'mauna-kea-vis.webp'} alt="The visitor station area on Mauna Kea at sunset: a railing, picnic tables and parked cars, a dark cinder cone, and a sea of clouds below a golden horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Visitor Station at 9,200 feet, 6:16 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cloud-sea.webp'} alt="Red cinder slopes and scrub falling away to a flat sea of white clouds under a pastel pink and blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sea of clouds, 6:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Mauna Kea deserves to be introduced properly. Measured from its base on the sea floor, it is the tallest mountain
    on Earth, more than 33,000 feet, though only 13,803 of them stand above the sea. To Native Hawaiians it is far more
    than a mountain. It is sacred, a place where the sky father Wākea and the earth mother Papahānaumoku meet, the home
    of the snow goddess Poliʻahu, and a burial place for honored ancestors. Its summit is also home to some of the
    world's great telescopes, and the long and painful dispute over building more of them is part of the mountain's
    story too. Visitors are asked to come with respect, and it is easy to see why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next to the station is a small cinder cone, Puʻukalepeamoa, with a trail up to an overlook, and as the sun went
    down it filled with people watching. To the west, the sun sank behind the long, low silhouette of Hualālai on the
    far side of the island, and the sky lit up in layers: deep orange at the horizon, then gold, then pale blue, with
    thin streaks of cloud catching fire overhead. Below, the cloud sea turned from white to gray.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hualalai-sunset.webp'} alt="A deep orange sunset behind a dark mountain silhouette, with streaks of cloud lit orange and a sea of cloud below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset behind Hualālai, 6:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunset-clouds.webp'} alt="Orange afterglow along the horizon above a dark mountainside, with a strip of low cloud in the valley." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The afterglow over the cloud layer.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Turning around is just as good. Behind the overlook, the mountain rises in slopes of red and brown cinder, dotted
    with little cones, each one a vent from an old eruption. In the last light the reds deepened, and the access road
    wound up the slope toward the summit, a thin gray line we would not be driving. The color of
    those slopes is the color of the iron in the cinder, oxidized to rust.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cinder-cone.webp'} alt="A red cinder cone on the dry slopes of Mauna Kea in dusk light, with brown mountain slopes rising behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Red cinder on the slopes of Mauna Kea.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'access-road.webp'} alt="The Mauna Kea Access Road winding across red and brown cinder slopes dotted with shrubs, with a pink-tinged sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The access road at dusk, 6:32 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    And then, at 6:34, the sky gave one last show. The high streaks of cloud, which had been gold, went a vivid
    salmon pink, all at once, as if somebody had turned up the saturation. The cone in front of us was black against
    them, and the cloud sea below was in shadow. It lasted about three minutes. Then the color drained out of the sky.
    At this altitude the temperature falls fast once the sun is gone, and night comes quickly in the tropics.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'afterglow.webp'} alt="Vivid salmon-pink clouds streaking across a twilight sky above an orange horizon, with a dark mountain silhouette and clouds below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The last color of the day, 6:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Milky Way</h3>
  <p>
    We waited for the dark. Mauna Kea is one of the best places on the planet to look at the sky, which is why the
    observatories are here. Its summit stands above about forty percent of the atmosphere and most of its water
    vapor, the clouds form a lid below that blocks the lights of the towns, and the county has rules to keep outdoor
    lighting on the island dim and amber for the astronomers' sake. By 7:24 the stars were out, and by half past seven
    the Milky Way was arching straight overhead.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'milky-way-1.webp'} alt="A dense field of stars with a pale band of the Milky Way crossing it, against a black sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Milky Way over Mauna Kea, 7:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    If you have only seen the Milky Way from a city, it is hard to describe the real thing. It isn't faint. It is a
    great pale river of light across the whole sky, lumpy and uneven, with dark lanes of dust running down its middle
    like a split in the current. There are so many stars around it that the familiar constellations are hard to find,
    lost in the crowd. In September the brightest part, toward the center of the galaxy in Sagittarius, is still up in
    the south after dark.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'milky-way-2.webp'} alt="The Milky Way's bright band with dark dust lanes running diagonally through a dense star field." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The dust lanes, 7:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'milky-way-3.webp'} alt="A tall view of a starry sky with the faint glowing band of the Milky Way crossing it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>7:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    These were all taken with a phone, held up to the sky in night mode, which is a small miracle in itself. The
    camera roll holds about thirty of them in twenty-five minutes, from 7:24 to 7:49, each one a long exposure and each
    one slightly different, as if taking enough of them might somehow capture the whole thing. None of them does, of
    course. But they are a decent record of the moment, and of how dark the sky gets at 9,200 feet above the Pacific.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then it was time to go. The drive down the mountain in the dark is slow, low gears and no lights except your own,
    and then it is the long road back across the Saddle and down to the coast. We pulled into Waikoloa just after nine,
    about twelve hours after the morning walk at the fishponds. Sea level, a crater, the clouds from above and the
    galaxy overhead, all in one day.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 4: Kona, and home</h2>
  <p>
    Sunday was for going home, slowly. There was one more lunch in <strong>Kailua-Kona</strong>, at a Chinese
    restaurant: a whole steamed fish under a pile of slivered ginger and scallion in soy sauce, on a red-and-gold
    plate. After three days of fried fish, grilled shrimp and pineapple boats, it was the perfect quiet ending. By two we were at the airport.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'steamed-fish.webp'} alt="A whole steamed fish topped with slivered ginger and scallions in soy sauce on a red and gold patterned platter." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A last lunch: steamed fish with ginger and scallion.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kona-airport-sculpture.webp'} alt="A large curling sculpture shaped like a wave, among palms and a lava-stone planter outside the Kona airport." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A wave sculpture outside the Kona airport, 2:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Kona airport is as good to leave as it is to arrive at. You wait outside, more or less, among palms and lawns
    and lava-stone walls, and the last thing we photographed on the island was a big curling sculpture by the
    departure area, shaped like a breaking wave. We flew out that afternoon and were home well after midnight. The souvenirs came
    out of the suitcase at three in the morning: six boxes of Hawaiian macadamia shortbread, in original, pineapple,
    lilikoʻi, brownie, taro and guava.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'macadamia-boxes.webp'} alt="Six colorful boxes of Hawaiian macadamia nut shortbread packed in an open suitcase, in original, pineapple, lilikoʻi, brownie, taro and guava flavors." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The haul: six flavors of macadamia shortbread.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Looking back</h2>
  <p>
    Four days is not enough for the Big Island. It is an island of five volcanoes and, by the usual count, most of
    the world's climate zones, and we spent most of our time driving between them. But the driving was part of it. Crossing the
    Saddle twice in two days, you see the whole island in cross-section: the lava coast, the high grassland, the
    rainforest, and back again. And Saturday was the kind of day you only get on an island like this, one that moves
    from ancient fishponds to a frozen lava lake to a sacred mountain above the clouds before dinner.
  </p>
  <p>
    A short trip forces choices. A year later I came back, for longer, and saw Kīlauea glowing red at night; that trip
    is <a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">here</a>. But this first visit left its own set of pictures: a gray crater floor
    through ʻōhiʻa branches, a stripe of orange above a sea of clouds, and the Milky Way overhead.
  </p>
